  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM EDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nDry weather persists through the start of the weekend, yet autumn \ntemperatures transition to late-summer warmth at times. High \npressure is now centered over the Steel City (PIT) permitting mild \nsoutherly flow to ease in. Aloft, on water vapor, an upper-level \nridge over CA and WA has buckled the jet stream through the Northern \nPlains and upper Midwest. This allows a progressive weather pattern \nto take shape this week and ultimately results in tug-of-war with \nhigh temperatures. A few spots south of US 24 may see highs in the \nlow-80s Wednesday afternoon, yet we'll cool back down about 8 to 10 \ndegrees on Thursday, only to warm back up toward 80 degrees \nSaturday.  \n\nAside from temperatures, eyes are also on the western Gulf where the \nNational Hurricane Center is monitoring an area for tropical cyclone \ndevelopment. Global model guidance suggests remnants from this \nemerging system could arrive to at least the southeast portions of \nthe forecast area late this weekend. Nothing to be alarmed about at \nthis distance with an in-house blend of global models suggesting \nabout a 30-40% chance of measurable rainfall. With a strong ridge \nbuilding over the Central US, a westward drift of the lingering rain \nshield appears unlikely. To the north, a frontal boundary pushing \ninto the northern Great Lakes this weekend will provide further \nsteering influence of any tropical remains. Incoming rain would \nprove inconvenient for harvest activities.",
